.TH TIMBERFS\-QUERY\-DOCUMENT 5 "2026" "timberfs" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
timberfs\-query\-document \- a whole timberfs search, as JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
timberfs query \-\-query FILE
timberfs query \-\-query \-            # from stdin
timberfs query ... \-\-dump\-json      # the document these flags describe
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A query document says what to search, over what window, for what, and in
what form the answer should come. It is the same value the command\-line
flags build \(em one question asked two ways, not two dialects of it \(em
and
.B \-\-dump\-json
prints the document any set of flags describes, which is the quickest way
to learn the format and the only description of it that cannot drift.
.PP
The document is meant to be generated. It is the input a tool, a client
library or a query server hands to timberfs, and the flags are the human
sugar for the common shapes.
.SH GRAMMAR
.nf
{
  "v": "1.0-EXPERIMENTAL",
  "stores": { "select": [ { "key": "type", "op": "=", "value": "console" } ] },
  "window": { "axis": "logline", "from": 1787727600000, "to": null,
              "from_chunk": null },
  "match":  { "granularity": "entries",
              "all": [ { "has": "ERROR" } ],
              "any": [ { "has": "timeout" } ] },
  "max":    { "entries": 1000 },
  "tail":   { "chunks": 10 },
  "response_format": { "kind": "loglines",
                       "options": { "no_filename": true } }
}
.fi
.PP
Only
.B v
and
.B stores
are required.
.SH MEMBERS
.TP
.B v
The format version, a
.B string
matched
.B exactly
against the one this build accepts. A string rather than a number so the
warning travels in the document: a generator that writes
.B 1.0\-EXPERIMENTAL
has typed the word. Its structure is meaningful to people and opaque to
timberfs \(em there is no parsing and no ordering, so
.B "1.0"
and
.B "1"
are simply different strings.
.TP
.B stores
Which stores to read, as a
.B select
predicate over their labels \(em the same one
.B \-\-select
takes, in structured form: a list of
.BR key ", " op ", " value
objects, ANDed, where
.B op
is
.BR = ", " != ", " =~ ", " !~ ", " =* " or " !* .
Regexes are anchored at both ends;
.B =*
is a LITERAL substring, which is what a person means by "the store with
apache in its name" and is not the same as
.BR =~.*apache.* ,
where a
.B .
in the text would be read as a pattern.
An absent label reads as the empty string, so
.B "service="
selects the stores that do not declare one.
.RS
.PP
⚠ An operator this timberfs does not know is REFUSED, naming the ones it
has. It is never guessed at, because a near miss cannot be guessed at
safely: the selector parser tries operators longest\-first, so an unknown
one is not rejected there \(em a shorter operator is found inside it and
the remainder becomes part of the VALUE. A mistyped
.B =?
reads as
.B =
against
.BR ?value ,
matching nothing; a mistyped
.B !=X
reads as
.B !=
against
.BR Xvalue ,
matching nearly every store. Both would be answered as though understood.
.PP
This is also what a NEWER generator meets on an OLDER timberfs, and the
reason to check the version rather than the answer:
.B =*
shipped after
.BR v0.23.1 ,
and a build without it read
.B name=*apache
as
.B name
equal to
.BR *apache .
The refusal is in this build; it cannot reach one already deployed. The
schema enumerates the operators so a generator can be told rather than
find out.
.PP
There is deliberately NO way to name a store by file path. A path is the
worst key a query can use: it is not stable across a move, it is not
unique across forests, and it says nothing about what the store holds. A
store is identified by what it IS \(em its labels \(em and the path is an
implementation detail of where a forest happens to keep it.
.PP
An empty or omitted
.B select
is every store, which is how a caller enumerates: the same request with
no predicate, not a second verb.
.PP
A
.B forests
list belongs here and is ABSENT rather than present\-and\-refused: a
contract that names a member the code ignores is the failure this format's
strictness exists to prevent. Adding an optional member later is allowed
within
.BR v=1 ,
so there is nothing to reserve.
.RE
.TP
.B window
.B axis
is
.B logline
(the timestamps the lines themselves carry) or
.B write
(when the data arrived), and it is
.B REQUIRED.
The flags do not make you choose \(em
.B \-\-from
means logline time in a windowed read and write time under
.B \-\-follow
\(em and that is a defect this format deliberately does not inherit.
.B from
and
.B to
are milliseconds since the epoch.
.B from_chunk
is where to START, by chunk number: a place on the tape rather than a
time, and exact where a timestamp can match two chunks that share a
boundary millisecond. It is unaffected by
.B axis
\(em that measures
.B from
and
.BR to ,
and a chunk number is not a clock.
.RS
.PP
It RESUMES a following read from the position a consumer's cursor holds,
and it SEEKS a bounded one \(em the same operation. Beside
.B "max: {chunks: 1}"
and
.B "response_format.kind: \(dqchunks\(dq"
it is a seek to exactly one chunk, which is what a pager asks for, and
each
.B chunk
record carries its own number back so a client can see where it landed.
.PP
A read has ONE start, so it is REFUSED beside anything else that names
one \(em
.BR from ,
a
.BR tail ,
a
.BR cursor .
Two starts have no rule for which wins, and picking one silently is how a
caller resumes somewhere it did not ask for. Everything that is not a
start composes: the window's
.BR to ,
the predicates, either axis.
.RE
.TP
.B match
Predicates, flat: every
.B all
must match, at least one
.B any
must, and no
.B none
may. Flat rather than a boolean tree \(em an arbitrary tree is expressible
in JSON but not in flags, and then
.B \-\-dump\-json
could not reproduce a server's query. That waits for a version 2.
.RS
.PP
A predicate sets EXACTLY ONE matcher. The contract says what a caller
wants to ASK, not what timberfs can answer cheaply:
.PP
.TP 12
.B has
A word\-anchored phrase. The form the token index holds, and the only one
that can skip a chunk whole.
.TP
.B substring
A literal anywhere, even inside a longer word. Rides the index on its
INTERIOR whole words when it has any.
.TP
.B regex
A regular expression, as given. Never rides the index.
.PP
.B caseless
compares without case, and can never ride the index, which is exact\-case.
It is refused on
.B regex
\(em a pattern says
.B (?i)
itself, and two ways to say it could disagree.
.PP
⚠ None of that changes what a predicate MEANS. The index decides how much
gets read, never what matches \(em so a
.B regex
over a whole store is slow, not wrong.
.RE
.RS
.PP
.B granularity
says what they SELECT, and it is
.B REQUIRED
whenever
.B match
is present \(em for the same reason
.B window.axis
is, and it exists because this format shipped without it:
.PP
.TP 12
.B entries
The entries that actually contain the terms. What a reader almost always
means. The index still skips chunks it can prove are irrelevant, so this
is not the slow option \(em it is the same read with the survivors judged
one entry at a time. An entry is a stamped line and its continuation
lines, so a stack trace matches or does not match as a whole.
.TP
.B chunks
The chunks that MAY contain them, emitted
.BR whole .
A superset, and the cheapest thing a store can answer: the index alone
decides it and nothing is decompressed to find out. Ask for it when the
next stage does its own matching.
.PP
The difference is not small. On a 1.2 GiB store a term appearing in five
entries selects 398 chunks and 325,767 lines.
.PP
A chunk sweep can only be narrowed by what the index can PROVE, so
.BR none ,
.B regex
and
.B caseless
are all
.B refused
with
.BR "granularity: chunks" ,
rather than parsing and narrowing nothing. The exclusion is the sharpest
of the three: a Bloom filter says a term MAY be in a chunk, never that it
is absent, so honouring
.B none
there would return exactly the chunks it was asked to remove.
.PP
.B entries
with
.B "response_format.kind: chunks"
is refused for the mirror reason: compressed chunks move verbatim, so
nothing decompresses them to judge an entry. The response echoes the granularity it applied, so a
consumer can tell a superset from an answer.
.RE
.TP
.BR max ", " tail
.B max
caps forward from the start;
.B tail
takes the last N. They are different operations rather than one with a
sign, so giving both is refused. Each needs
.B exactly one unit,
because "stop after 10" of WHAT decides both the answer and its cost:
.RS
.TP 12
.B entries
A full read \(em chunks are decompressed and framed to know where one
entry ends.
.TP
.B chunks
Nearly free: the index knows how many there are, so a
.B tail
of N is arithmetic and no chunk is read to find the boundary. This is the
unit to bound a
.B chunk\-granular
search in \(em an entry count there caps entries nobody asked about, so
"the first 5 entries" of a chunk sweep can easily contain none of the
matches.
.PP
Neither unit nor both is refused rather than guessed at. A
.B bytes
unit belongs here and is absent until something produces one; a
.B scope
(total versus per\-store) likewise. Counts are totals across stores, so a
fleet view capped at 5 chunks reads five, not five per store \(em and
because stores are read one after another, those five come from the
first store(s) reached. The
.B position
records say which, and the
.B cursor
resumes there.
.RE
.TP
.B deadline
How LONG the search may take:
.BR "{ \(dqms\(dq: 5000 }" .
It COMPOSES with
.BR max ,
rather than conflicting with it, because they bound different things \(em
a fleet read is slow because it READS a lot, not because it matches a
lot, so no count bounds the wait.
.RS
.PP
The point is that it is answered rather than abandoned. A timeout in the
caller drops the connection and everything that had already arrived on
it; a deadline returns the work done, and the response says exactly how
far it got:
.PP
.TP 4
\(bu
a store read to the end has
.B chunks_read
equal to
.BR chunks_selected ;
.TP
\(bu
the store the deadline stopped inside has fewer, and a
.B position
to resume from;
.TP
\(bu
a store selected but never opened has
.B chunks_read=0
beside a non\-zero
.BR chunks_selected ;
.TP
\(bu
and a store the deadline reached before SELECTION did has no
.B source
record at all, though
.B stream\-start
still counts it in
.BR sources .
.PP
.B stream\-end
carries
.B limit=deadline
with
.BR status=limited .
Hand the positions back as a
.B cursor
and the next request continues.
.PP
Zero is refused: it reads as \(lqanswer instantly\(rq and means \(lqread
nothing\(rq. To ask what a search WOULD cover, use
.B response_format.kind: \(dqstores\(dq
instead.
.PP
⚠ Only a
.B records
answer can report that the deadline fired. On the other kinds it still
applies \(em it bounds work, not framing \(em but the marker is a note on
standard error, and a relay is unlikely to carry that.
.RE
.TP
.B Every answer says what produced it
A JSON answer is an object carrying
.B server_version
beside what was asked for:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{ \(dqserver_version\(dq: \(dqtimberfs, 0.24.0\(dq,
  \(dqstores\(dq: [ ... ] }
.fi
.PP
The records stream carries the same field in
.BR stream\-start .
.B loglines
and
.B chunks
have nowhere to put it, which is what makes them the raw kinds.
.PP
It rides on the ANSWER rather than on a request of its own. A client that
must ask separately is a client that can forget to, and the version is
wanted exactly when something looks wrong \(em which is while reading an
answer, not before it.
.PP
Product AND version, because the thing answering need not be a timberfs:
a relay, a wrapper or another implementation says what it is in the same
field, the way an HTTP
.B Server:
header does.
.PP
The reason it exists is a defect it would have caught.
.B =*
shipped after
.BR v0.23.1 ,
and a build without it does not refuse the operator \(em it truncates it
to
.B =
and reads the
.B *
as part of the value, so
.B name=*apache
silently became
.B name
equal to
.BR *apache :
an empty answer, exit 0, for a store the same session had just listed.
.PP
The answer's contract is published too, at
.IR /usr/share/doc/timberfs/query\-answer.schema.json ,
generated from the same types and checked against them by a test \(em so
a client can validate what came back, not only be refused for what it
sent.
.PP
⚠ A VERSION, not a list of what this build can do. A capability list
would be a second source of truth that can disagree with it \(em two
builds both calling themselves 0.24.0 could advertise different lists,
and then neither says which build it is. The version is the identity;
what follows from it is the client's to know.
.RE
.TP
.B cursor
Where a previous answer stopped, per store: a list of
.BR id / offset
objects, which is exactly the shape of the
.B position
records that answer ended with. A client hands back what it was given.
.RS
.PP
The offset is absolute on that store's
.BR tape :
what has ever left the store, plus where the next undelivered entry sits
in what remains. Retention cannot move it \(em
.B remove_head
rebases a chunk's own offset and grows the drop counter by exactly as
much \(em and, unlike a clock, it cannot COLLIDE. That is not a corner
case: on a fleet whose entries share a second, resuming by timestamp
loses every entry that shared the last one.
.PP
Include an entry for every store the previous answer EXAMINED, including
the ones that matched nothing. Leave those out and the next page rescans
them from the start of the window, which on a fleet is most of the cost.
.PP
Hand back what you were given, unaltered. An entry with NO
.B offset
means the start of the window, and a
.B position
carries none only where a store has been consumed nothing of \(em one
that merely went quiet on a page reports the offset it was RESUMED from,
so a walk cannot lose its place by paging past a store that had nothing
to say.
.PP
A store ABSENT from the cursor is read from the start of the window. For
a tail that is right \(em it is new, and everything it holds is new. A
bounded walk that wants otherwise pins its store set instead.
.RE
.SS Paging, and the ceilings it pages under
.B stream\-end
says whether a cap stopped the read
.RB ( status=limited ,
naming which one), a
.B position
record per store says where each one got to, and
.B cursor
hands those back. That is a page, and
.B deadline
bounds how LONG it may take rather than how much it returns.
.PP
A document is a request from somewhere else, and the machine answering
it declares its own CEILINGS on what one may ask for \(em
.BR /etc/timberfs/limits.conf ,
.BR timberfs (1).
They are announced rather than discovered: every answer with somewhere
to put them carries them, so a caller sizes its pages before it asks.
The
.B stores
answer has a
.B limits
object beside
.BR server_version ,
and a records or chunks
.B stream-start
has
.BR limits.max.entries ,
.B limits.max.chunks
and
.B limits.deadline_ms
as fields. Absent means no ceiling.
.PP
A
.B max
or
.B deadline
over the ceiling is LOWERED, not refused: the answer is then the first
page of the one that was asked for, and
.B stream-end
names which bound stopped it \(em
.B limit=limits.max.entries
where the request's own bound would have said
.BR limit=max.entries .
A
.B tail
over it is REFUSED, because a tail answer carries no
.B position
to continue from: a shorter tail is a different answer rather than the
start of the one asked for.
.PP
A page is not the only way to move. A cursor is a place in an ANSWER,
where
.B window.from_chunk
is a place in the STORE \(em so walking a result set and opening the log
at a coordinate are different motions, and they do not share a handle.
.TP
.B response_format
A
.B kind
plus
.BR options ,
not a bare name, because the options are orthogonal to the kind. Kinds:
.B loglines
(the log as a person reads it),
.B records
(the typed stream \(em see
.BR timberfs\-records (5)),
.B chunks
(compressed chunks verbatim, nothing decompressed at either end \(em
framed as
.BR timberfs\-records (5),
one
.B chunk
record per chunk, each carrying its ring),
.B stores
(the matched stores themselves, with no entries read).
.RS
.PP
.B stores
answers the store half of the document and stops there \(em what a fleet
view needs before it knows which store to read, and what an enumeration
is. Each match is a
.B store object,
exactly the one
.B "timberfs info \-\-json"
and
.B "timberfs list \-\-json"
emit \(em see
.B THE STORE OBJECT
in
.BR timberfs (1).
That object is NOT covered by this format's
.B EXPERIMENTAL
marker: the search may change under a caller, the stores it answers with
will not. Because it reads no entries, the members that describe entries
(\c
.BR window ", " match ", " max ", " tail )
are REFUSED with it rather than ignored.
.PP
.B window.axis
and the kind are not fully independent:
.B write
time with an entry kind, or
.B logline
time with
.BR chunks ,
name an axis the answer cannot honour, and are refused rather than
accepted and dropped.
.RE
.SH OMISSION WIDENS
A member left out
.B widens
the search rather than emptying it. A document naming only
.B stores
is "every entry of those stores". This is the single rule a generator must
get right: invert it and a member somebody forgot returns nothing, which
looks exactly like a correct empty result.
.SH STRICTNESS
An unknown member is an
.BR error .
A misspelled
.B "form"
for
.B "from"
must not become a silently unbounded search \(em a query language that
ignores a typo returns wrong results that look right, and laxness cannot
be tightened once callers depend on it. The refusal names the members
that were expected, so the error doubles as a description of the format.
.PP
⚠ This is the OPPOSITE of the rule in
.BR timberfs\-records (5),
and deliberately so. That format is a
.B response:
a consumer should tolerate a producer that grew a field, so unknown keys
are ignored there. This one is a
.B request,
where tolerating an unknown key means doing something other than what was
asked. Same project, two formats, opposite rules, each right for its
direction.
.SH VERSIONING
⚠ While the accepted version string carries
.BR EXPERIMENTAL ,
.B the format may be broken in place without the string changing.
A superseded version is not kept working; expect a generator and the
timberfs it talks to to be upgraded together.
.PP
That costs less than it sounds, because strictness does most of the
detecting already. Against a build that has moved on, an old generator
gets a specific error rather than a wrong answer: a removed or renamed
member is an
.BR "unknown field" ,
a newly required one is a
.BR "missing field" ,
a changed type is a type error.
.PP
The one change that earns a new version string even here is the one
nothing catches:
.B a member that keeps its name and its type but changes meaning
\(em
.B from
in seconds where it was milliseconds,
.B entries
counting chunks. That parses, and answers a different question than the
caller asked, with nothing anywhere saying so.
.PP
A document whose
.B v
this build does not accept is refused rather than partly interpreted, and
the refusal names both strings and whether the accepted one is
experimental.
.PP
When the format settles the string loses its marker, and the ordinary rule
starts: within a version, new members are
.B optional
and existing members never change meaning or type; anything else is a new
version.
.SH EXAMPLES
The document for a search you already know how to express:
.PP
.nf
timberfs query /var/log/timberfs/app/app.log \\
    \-\-from 2026\-08\-26 \-\-has ERROR \-\-max 10 \-\-dump\-json
.fi
.PP
and the same search, run from it:
.PP
.nf
timberfs query \-\-query search.json
timberfs query ... \-\-dump\-json | timberfs query \-\-query \-
.fi
.SH THE SCHEMA
A JSON Schema for this document is published at
.I /usr/share/doc/timberfs/query\-document.schema.json
and in the repository as
.IR docs/query\-document.schema.json .
It is a committed file rather than something a build produces, so a change
to the contract appears in a diff and is reviewed; a test fails if it and
the code disagree. It describes only what is IMPLEMENTED \(em members that
do not work yet are absent from both.
.PP
⚠ It describes
.B the version this build accepts,
not "the format". A build that accepts more than one version will publish
one schema per version, and this path will name the current one \(em so
treat it as a moving target and read
.B v
from it rather than assuming which version you have.
.SH EXAMPLES OF THE FORMAT
Worked examples ship at
.IR /usr/share/doc/timberfs/query\-examples/ ,
with a
.I README.md
naming what each one demonstrates, and in the repository as
.IR docs/examples/ .
The schema says what is LEGAL; those say what is USEFUL. Run one with:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs query \-\-query /usr/share/doc/timberfs/query\-examples/query\-windowed\-error.json
.RE
.fi
.SH NOT EXPRESSIBLE HERE
.B A live tail.
.B timberfs query \-\-follow
holds a stream open, where a document describes ONE search \(em and a
search that never ends is a subscription, which is shaped by whatever
protocol serves the document rather than by the document.
.B \-\-dump\-json
therefore REFUSES
.B \-\-follow
rather than quietly dropping it, which is how a caller ends up running a
different query than the one it printed.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-follow
on the machine that holds the store. A document's equivalent is to ask
again from where the last answer stopped, which is what
.B cursor
is for \(em the subscription is then the caller's loop rather than the
document's.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
